    "readTime": "5 min read",
    "category": "Buyer Guide",
    "content": "\n      <p>Ghana's second hand phone market is booming. With smartphones becoming essential for work, business and daily life, more Ghanaians are looking for affordable options. Here's your complete guide to finding the best deals on used phones in Ghana.</p>\n\n      <h2>1. Online Marketplaces (Best Option)</h2>\n      <p>Online marketplaces like <strong>Makola Digital</strong> have become the go-to destination for buying second hand phones in Ghana. You can browse hundreds of listings from verified sellers across Accra, Kumasi and beyond — all from the comfort of your home.</p>\n      <p>Benefits of buying online:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Compare prices from multiple sellers instantly</li>\n        <li>See clear photos of the actual phone</li>\n        <li>Chat directly with the seller on WhatsApp</li>\n        <li>Find deals from sellers across all 16 regions</li>\n        <li>No pressure to buy — browse at your own pace</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>2. Accra Mall & Shopping Centers</h2>\n      <p>Several phone shops around Accra Mall, Westfield Junction and East Legon sell certified refurbished phones. Prices are usually higher than online but you can inspect the phone in person before buying.</p>\n\n      <h2>3. Okaishie Market</h2>\n      <p>Okaishie in central Accra is Ghana's most famous electronics market. You'll find hundreds of phone vendors selling everything from budget Android phones to premium iPhones. Always negotiate — prices are rarely fixed here.</p>\n      <p>Tips for shopping at Okaishie:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Go early in the morning for the best selection</li>\n        <li>Always test the phone thoroughly before paying</li>\n        <li>Check the IMEI number to confirm it's not stolen</li>\n        <li>Negotiate — you can usually get 10-20% off the asking price</li>\n        <li>Bring someone knowledgeable about phones if possible</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>4. Kantamanto Market</h2>\n      <p>Kantamanto is known for second hand goods and you can find affordable phones here. However, quality varies significantly so inspect carefully and test everything before buying.</p>\n\n      <h2>5. Social Media Groups</h2>\n      <p>Facebook groups like \"Buy & Sell Ghana\" and WhatsApp groups have active phone traders. However, be careful of scams — always meet in a public place and never send money before seeing the phone.</p>\n\n      <h2>What to Check Before Buying a Second Hand Phone</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Battery health</strong> — should be above 80%</li>\n        <li><strong>Screen condition</strong> — check for cracks, dead pixels or burn-in</li>\n        <li><strong>IMEI number</strong> — dial *#06# and verify it matches the box</li>\n        <li><strong>Network unlock</strong> — confirm it works with your SIM card</li>\n        <li><strong>Camera</strong> — test front and back cameras</li>\n        <li><strong>Charging port</strong> — test with your charger</li>\n        <li><strong>Buttons and speakers</strong> — test all physical buttons and audio</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Best Second Hand Phones to Buy in Ghana Right Now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>iPhone 11</strong> — great value, reliable performance, GHS 1,500-2,500</li>\n        <li><strong>Samsung Galaxy S21</strong> — excellent camera, GHS 2,000-3,500</li>\n        <li><strong>iPhone 12</strong> — 5G ready, good battery, GHS 2,500-4,000</li>\n        <li><strong>Tecno Camon series</strong> — affordable, good for Ghana network, GHS 800-1,500</li>\n        <li><strong>Samsung A series</strong> — reliable mid-range option, GHS 900-2,000</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Find Your Next Phone on Makola Digital</h2>\n      <p>Browse hundreds of second hand phones listed by verified sellers across Ghana on Makola Digital. Compare prices, chat with sellers on WhatsApp and find the best deal — all for free.</p>\n    "
